      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U11", "U10"],
      "pins": ["U11.GND1", "U11.GND2", "U10.GND1", "U10.GND2"],
      "nets": ["GND"],
      "observed": "The isolated CAN transceivers U11 (CAN0) and U10 (CAN1) have both of their isolated ground pins, GND1 and GND2, connected to the same common GND net.",
      "expected": "For an isolated CAN transceiver/bus, the input-side and output-side grounds should be separate nets to preserve galvanic isolation; the specification describes the board as using isolated CAN buses through isolated CAN transceivers.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "CAN BUS sheet (page 7): U11 has GND1 and GND2 pins both connected to GND symbols; CAN BUS sheet (page 11): U10 similarly has GND1 and GND2 connected to GND"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller (U8, U9) through an isolated CAN transceiver (U11/companion)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The CAN buses would not be galvanically isolated as intended, increasing susceptibility to ground loops and noise on the motor/actuator CAN interfaces.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The drawing contains at least one likely design deviation: the +12V boost feedback divider uses 1.8M/20k, inconsistent with a 12V output, and the isolated CAN transceivers also tie both isolated grounds to common GND, defeating the stated isolation."
}

